WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.820
How can one kinase carry two different orders?

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:12.180
In a rat cell line called PC12, epidermal growth factor, or EGF, says divide.

3
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:17.640
Nerve growth factor, or NGF, says stop dividing and grow nerve-like processes.

4
00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.080
Both switch on the same kinase, ERK.

5
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.980
The molecule is the same, so the difference must lie in something else about the

6
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:25.980
signal.

7
00:00:26.283 --> 00:00:30.163
EGF switches ERK on briefly, and the cell divides.

8
00:00:31.063 --> 00:00:36.063
NGF keeps ERK on much longer, and the cell stops dividing and grows processes.

9
00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:39.563
Engineered cells carry extra EGF receptors.

10
00:00:40.303 --> 00:00:44.343
Given EGF, they keep ERK active for longer, and they differentiate.

11
00:00:44.343 --> 00:00:48.183
So length matters, and the pathway's wiring shapes it.

12
00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:50.777
A cell has no stopwatch.

13
00:00:51.357 --> 00:00:55.237
What it can do is compare the signal with a delayed product of that same signal.

14
00:00:55.857 --> 00:00:59.697
In fibroblasts, ERK switches on a gene called c-fos.

15
00:01:00.357 --> 00:01:04.477
The c-Fos protein takes time to make, and once made it is quickly destroyed.

16
00:01:05.137 --> 00:01:09.377
So the test is simple: is ERK still on when the new protein appears?

17
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.660
Think of a clinic that calls your name a while after you sign in.

18
00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.380
Only the people still in the waiting room are seen.

19
00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:22.220
If ERK is still active when c-Fos appears, phosphates are added near one end of the

20
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:27.480
protein. ERK adds them, and so does a kinase that ERK activates.

21
00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:29.940
Those phosphates protect c-Fos.

22
00:01:30.983 --> 00:01:34.983
After a brief signal, ERK is already off when c-Fos appears.

23
00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:37.423
So c-Fos is made and lost.

24
00:01:38.143 --> 00:01:42.183
After a long signal, ERK is still on, and c-Fos builds up.

25
00:01:42.783 --> 00:01:45.943
As a transcription factor, it can then switch on later genes.

26
00:01:46.763 --> 00:01:51.063
So in fibroblasts, c-Fos lets the cell tell a brief signal from a long one.

27
00:01:52.017 --> 00:01:56.817
The signal reaches c-Fos by a fast route and a slow route, and c-Fos needs both.

28
00:01:57.397 --> 00:02:01.797
It needs phosphates from ERK, and it needs the new protein, which takes time to

29
00:02:01.797 --> 00:02:06.937
make. This was shown for c-Fos in fibroblasts, and later for several other early

30
00:02:06.937 --> 00:02:10.817
gene products. Do not assume every long signal is read this way.

31
00:02:10.817 --> 00:02:14.557
Keep this rule: a part that arrives late can time a signal.
